{
  "root_cause": "The ArrowRight keydown handler in tab.js updates the aria-selected state of the next tab but does not move focus to it, causing focus to remain on the previous tab or the body after the key press.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify the _keydown method in js/src/tab.js to focus the newly activated tab after invoking its show() method, ensuring keyboard navigation moves focus alongside selection.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "js/src/tab.js",
      "sha256": "69ca6e1d090610fe209a5b54a151e20b738214aabf1464807c85c17c0e29848c",
      "before": "  _keydown(event) {
    if (!([ARROW_LEFT_KEY, ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_UP_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key))) {
      return
    }

    event.stopPropagation()// stopPropagation/preventDefault both added to support up/down keys without scrolling the page
    event.preventDefault()
    const isNext = [ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key)
    const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren().filter(element => !isDisabled(element)), event.target, isNext, true)

    if (nextActiveElement) {
      Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show()
    }
  }",
      "after": "  _keydown(event) {
    if (!([ARROW_LEFT_KEY, ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_UP_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key))) {
      return
    }

    event.stopPropagation()// stopPropagation/preventDefault both added to support up/down keys without scrolling the page
    event.preventDefault()
    const isNext = [ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key)
    const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren().filter(element => !isDisabled(element)), event.target, isNext, true)

    if (nextActiveElement) {
      Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show()
      nextActiveElement.focus()
    }
  }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Arrow key navigation will now move focus to the next tab, making the focused tab match the selected tab and satisfying keyboard operability requirements (WCAG 2.1.1, 2.4.3).",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Focusing the tab after show() could interfere if show() already focuses the element in future versions.",
    "If a tab panel contains an autofocus element, moving focus to the tab first may briefly shift focus away from that element, but this is acceptable as the tab is the entry point.",
    "Mouse click behavior remains unchanged because clicking a button already focuses it."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Pressing ArrowRight from a tab moves focus to the next tab and updates its aria-selected to true.",
    "Pressing ArrowLeft from a tab moves focus to the previous tab and updates its aria-selected to true.",
    "Focus loops correctly from the last tab to the first and vice versa.",
    "Tab panels show/hide correctly when tabs are changed via arrow keys.",
    "Mouse clicks on tabs continue to work as expected.",
    "No JavaScript errors are thrown during keyboard navigation."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Frozen journey requirements:
Step 0: seek #tab-account (initial)
Then step 1: ArrowRight -> expects focus moves to #tab-notifications and aria-selected becomes true.
But they got VERIFIED_FAIL because after pressing ArrowRight, focus remained on body (focus assertion failed). Actually they had two assertions: attribute aria-selected on #tab-notifications true (passed) and focus on #tab-notifications (failed). So focus didn't move to the button.

Second step: another ArrowRight expecting focus on #tab-billing etc also fails.

Thus issue: keydown ArrowRight handling not moving focus to next tab.

Looking at observed state after first ArrowRight: In "after" snapshot, we see the HTML shows that #tab-account still has aria-selected="false"? Wait after snippet: It shows:

<ul class="nav nav-tabs mt-3" id="settings-tabs" role="tablist">
    <li class="nav-item" role="presentation"><button class="nav-link" id="tab-account" ... aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Account</button></li>
    <li class="nav-item" role="presentation"><button class="nav-link active" id="tab-notifications" ... aria-selected="true">Notifications</button></li>
    ...

So the ARIA selected updated correctly (Notifications active). However focus remained on body (the focus is on body?). They said focus assertion failed: expected focus on #tab-notifications but observed focus on body.

In the "before" snapshot, focus was on #tab-account (active). After pressing ArrowRight, they expected focus to move to #tab-notifications, but it didn't.

Observation: In the keyEvents array: they recorded a keydown Tab then keydown ArrowRight on #tab-account with defaultPrevented:true. That suggests that the ArrowRight keydown was prevented (maybe by bootstrap tab JS preventing default to avoid page scroll?), but they also stopped propagation. In tab.js _keydown they do event.stopPropagation(); event.preventDefault(); then compute nextActiveElement and call show() on it.

But why didn't focus move? Possibly because the nextActiveElement returned null or not a tab? Let's examine _getChildren and filtering.

In tab.js: _getChildren returns SelectorEngine.find(SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM, this._parent). SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM is defined as:

const SELECTOR_INNER = `.nav-link${NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE}, .list-group-item${NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE}, [role=\"tab\"]${NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE}`
const SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE = '[data-bs-toggle=\"tab\"], [data-bs-toggle=\"pill\"], [data-bs-toggle=\"list\"]'
const SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM = `${SELECTOR_INNER}, ${SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE}`

Given our markup: buttons have class nav-link, role tab, data-bs-toggle="tab". They also have NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE which is ':not(.dropdown-toggle)'. Since they are not dropdown-toggle, they match.

Thus _getChildren should return the three tab buttons.

Then _keydown filters children with !isDisabled(element). None disabled.

Then getNextActiveElement(list, event.target, isNext, true). event.target is #tab-account (the button). isNext true for ArrowRight.

getNextActiveElement: It finds index of activeElement in list, then increments/decrements, with cycling allowed.

Should work.

But maybe the issue is that after calling show() on nextActiveElement, the focus is not moved because show() does not focus the tab. In bootstrap tab JS, showing a tab does not automatically focus it; it just activates it (adds active class, sets aria-selected true, removes tabindex -1? Actually look at _activate: When activating a tab (element with role tab), they do:

element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_ACTIVE)
this._activate(getElementFromSelector(element)) // activate panel
const complete = () => {
  if (element.getAttribute('role') !== 'tab') {
    element.classList.add(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)
    return
  }

  element.removeAttribute('tabindex')
  element.setAttribute('aria-selected', true)
  this._toggleDropDown(element, true)
  EventHandler.trigger(element, EVENT_SHOWN, { relatedTarget: relatedElem })
}

So for a tab (role===tab), they remove tabindex (so it becomes focusable?) Actually they remove tabindex attribute entirely (which means it becomes focusable according to its tabindex default? Removing tabindex makes it follow default: if it's not focusable naturally (like a div) it becomes not focusable; but a button is naturally focusable, so removing tabindex should keep it focusable. They also set aria-selected true. They do not call element.focus().

Thus after activating the next tab, focus remains where it was (previous tab) unless we manually focus it. The expected behavior for arrow key navigation is to move focus to the next tab. So we need to focus the newly activated tab.

Thus fix: In _keydown after calling show() on nextActiveElement, we should also focus that element.

Alternatively modify _activate to focus the tab when activating a tab (role===tab). But that might affect other usage (click activation). However clicking a tab also should focus it? Usually clicking a tab focuses it (since button gets focus). But bootstrap's tab.js does not focus on click either; the native button click will focus it because it's a button. Actually clicking a button will cause focus on the button (unless prevented). So for mouse click, focus is already on the button. For keyboard navigation, we need to move focus manually.

Thus we can adjust _keydown: after calling show(), we can focus the nextActiveElement.

Let's look at the _keydown code:

if (nextActiveElement) {
  Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show()
}

We need to add focus.

Potential fix: after show(), call nextActiveElement.focus().

But note that show() may be asynchronous due to fade transition; they use _queueCallback. However focusing immediately may be okay; but if there is a fade transition, the panel may not be shown yet but focus can still be on tab. That's fine.

Thus edit tab.js: add focus.

